<TopicPage slug="subjects/dental-hygiene/foundational/the-sciences-behind-hygiene">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Dental hygiene is prevention as a licensed profession. Its sciences — oral anatomy,
    histology, microbiology and immunology — explain why the mouth develops disease and why the
    hygienist's interventions work. The flagship example is fluoride: a simple ion whose chemistry
    reshapes the daily battle between demineralisation and repair.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the caries battle and fluoride's two roles" widget="equationStep" config={fluorideCfg} />

  <h2>Head and neck anatomy is working knowledge</h2>
  <p>
    The hygienist instruments around nerves, vessels and sinuses. Knowing the trigeminal nerve's
    branches explains where anaesthesia goes; knowing the lymph nodes explains what to palpate;
    knowing salivary gland ducts explains where stones hide. Anatomy in this profession is not an
    exam hurdle — it is the map of every appointment.
  </p>

  <h2>The microbiology of the mouth</h2>
  <p>
    The oral cavity hosts hundreds of species in structured biofilms. Streptococcus mutans drives
    caries; Porphyromonas gingivalis anchors periodontitis. The hygienist's mechanical disruption
    (scaling, brushing) is biofilm management — which is why it must be repeated daily by the
    patient and regularly in the chair: biofilms regrow on schedule.
  </p>

  <h2>Immunology explains the destruction</h2>
  <p>
    Periodontitis is not the bacteria eating ligament directly — it is the host's inflammatory
    response to them doing the damage. That reframing explains why some people with heavy plaque
    lose no bone and others with modest plaque lose teeth: the immune response is the variable,
    and it is why smoking and diabetes (both immune-modulating) are the great risk multipliers.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> CODA accreditation standards; Nield-Gehrig, <em>Fundamentals of Periodontal Instrumentation</em>; Darby &amp; Walsh, <em>Dental Hygiene: Theory and Practice</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
